  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 144 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThursday through Tuesday:\n\nAn upper air ridge will be moving towards the region on Thursday\nbringing colder and drier air from Canada. Surface winds will\nstart from the Northwest and shift to be from the northeast by\nFriday morning with light winds expected all day. High\ntemperatures in the low 70s/upper 80s inland and low to mid 70s\nnear the shoreline. Low temperatures will be near the upper 50s \nand lower 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the region on Friday.\nWinds will shift to be from the southeast which will bring in\nwarmer and more moist air from the south near the surface with\ncold/dry air still being advected from the north. Will see high\nand low temperatures similar to Thursday.\n\nThe next low pressure system will start to move in on Saturday\nfrom the Great Plains. Precip chances will start late Saturday\nevening (20-30% chance). With an elongated trough expected to \nmove over the region on Sunday. PoPs will increase with a 30-50% \nchance for precipitation. With warm and moist air near the surface\nand cold/dry air aloft along with SBCAPE near 1500J/kg, there is \na chance to see storms within this system. Winds will be from the \nsouthwest with gusts around 15 knots throughout the region with \nhigher gusts expected within storms.\n\nA secondary trough will move through the CWA on Monday which can \nbring additional precipitation chances (30-40) with a possibility \nfor there to be a storm or two. An additional shortwave will move \nover the region on Tuesday, but rain chances with this system will\nbe even smaller (15-30%) along with a decreased chance for storms\ncompared to previous couple of days.\n\nSoergel/CMiller",
